environmental ethics, environmentalism, and other fields. The dualism results
in problematic uses of the term nature in Western society.
Nature may be:
Objectified for study and use, or, in other words, understood through
subject (human)-object (nature) relationships;
Regarded as primitive or backward;
Regarded as unfeeling and unknowing;
Regarded as subordinate in status;
Regarded as the source of base human impulses and temptations;
Associated with Indigenous people (and such people may be regarded as
inferior);
Regarded as the world of the profane as contrasted with the divine;
Regarded as the environment of humans;
Regarded as dirty, threatening, obdurate, and/or stingy—a source of
hardships and catastrophe—and thus something to be dominated,
controlled, and overcome;
Regarded as lacking worth, especially lacking economic worth absent
human improvement and development;
Associated with women or more generally the feminine and as being
inferior to men or more generally the masculine, with both nature and
women being subject to abuse; and
Regarded as limiting or, conversely, as boundless.29
While Earth lawyers as a pragmatic matter will need to distinguish humans
and nature in many situations, when they do so, they should endeavor to
avoid misunderstandings based on culturally inscribed notions of nature-
culture dualism.
